No. Standing alone, “protect neighborhood character” establishes the planning director’s stated objective; it does not establish that preservation was a pretext for excluding working-class families of color. The phrase is ambiguous and commonly appears in legitimate preservation and planning discussions. Its evidentiary weight depends on context.

**Pretext** means the stated preservation rationale was not the actual rationale. Evidence that would materially support that conclusion includes:

- the memo defining “neighborhood character” through residents’ race, class, tenure, or opposition to affordable housing;
- communications linking the overlay to stopping this project or its intended residents;
- initiating or changing the overlay after officials learned who would occupy the housing;
- applying preservation criteria to this site while exempting comparable properties;
- departures from normal procedures without a documented preservation reason;
- preservation assessments showing the property did not qualify under the city’s stated standards;
- shifting or factually false explanations from officials;
- demographic or housing-impact evidence combined with evidence of discriminatory intent.

Disparate impact alone can matter legally, but it does not by itself prove intentional bad faith. The relevant legal standards also depend on the claim and jurisdiction.

You can use the memo accurately and forcefully:

> Before the fault audit existed, the planning director described the overlay as a tool to “protect neighborhood character.” The council must explain what “character” meant, why this site was selected, which comparable properties were treated differently, and whether stopping this housing project was an intended result. The later geological finding cannot answer those questions or retroactively supply the overlay’s original rationale.

If the surrounding memo connects “neighborhood character” to excluding this housing or its expected residents, that context could justify a stronger conclusion. On the quoted phrase alone, I would not call bad faith established.
